    body: [
      'Pipe Connect bolted pipes are dark pieces that cannot be rotated. They are part of the puzzle’s fixed layout, so treat them as clues rather than obstacles to click repeatedly. Water starts at the brass tap and must reach every drain. Some pipes are decoys and do not need water, which means the goal is to complete the required network, not to make every visible piece glow blue.',
      '@image:0',
      '## Trace the route from both ends',
      '### Fixed pieces narrow the route',
      'Begin at the tap and note which direction its opening faces. Then inspect each drain and work backward from its open connection. A route is complete only when adjacent pieces both open toward each other. A pipe that points toward a neighbor does not connect if that neighbor faces a different direction.',
      'Fixed pieces narrow the possible routes. A bolted corner pipe can only carry water through the sides it already exposes. Use that information to rule out neighboring arrangements before spending time rotating other pieces. When a fixed section connects two parts of the board, it may determine the only viable path between them.',
      '## Separate required pipes from decoys',
      'A pipe piece does not have to carry water just because it is on the board. Focus first on connecting the tap to every drain. If a branch does not lead to a drain or help connect another required section, it may be a decoy. This distinction prevents a common time sink: trying to make every loose piece part of a complete network.',
      'Look for paths that end at a drain and compare their possible entrances with the board’s fixed pieces. A drain needs water from a compatible neighboring pipe. Work backward from that requirement, then see whether the remaining route can reach the tap. If two drains depend on one central connection, make sure the middle pieces support both branches.',
      '@image:1',
      '## Use water flow as feedback',
      'Pipes that carry water turn blue, so the current flow shows which part of the arrangement is connected to the tap. If a segment stays dry, check the first boundary between blue and unlit pieces. One connection may point the wrong way, or a gap may separate two otherwise correct sections. Fixing the first break is more useful than rotating a pipe at random.',
      'The game clock starts on your first turn. Before that first rotation, take a short look at the tap, drains and bolted pieces. On later levels, you may need to reach several drains on a larger board, so planning the likely route before the timer begins can save hurried corrections. Right-clicking or using Shift+Space rotates a piece anticlockwise when that is faster than cycling clockwise.',
      '## Rotate around fixed pieces deliberately',
      'A rotating pipe usually has a small set of possible orientations. Compare each orientation with the neighboring openings and the route you need. If the piece sits at the edge, the board boundary removes some possibilities. If it sits beside a bolted piece, the fixed opening constrains it further. Set those constrained pieces early, then work inward.',
      'A hint turns one pipe in the right direction and bolts it down. That can establish one part of the route, but it does not automatically solve the rest of the board. Follow the new blue flow and use the hint as a confirmed anchor. If you undo a rotation, remember the timer is not refunded, so avoid using undo to explore every orientation.',
      '## Start with the hardest constraints',
      'A bolted piece beside a board edge is often more informative than a loose piece in the center. The edge limits which openings can be used, and the fixed orientation removes the option to rotate. Check whether the tap or a drain can connect through that piece. If so, its neighbor must face the matching direction. Set these forced relationships before working on flexible pipes.',
      'Then trace any branch that must serve more than one drain. A junction can split the flow, while a straight segment cannot. If the board has several possible routes, compare how many uncommitted pieces each route would require. The route with fewer uncertain rotations is often easier to finish before the timer expires, especially when the fixed pieces already define part of the network.',
      'On a board with multiple drains, do not stop after the first one turns blue. Continue tracing from each drain back toward the tap and check where the branches meet. A pipe may carry water to one drain while leaving another isolated. The visible flow helps you locate the first disconnected segment, and the bolted pieces can show whether a proposed junction is possible at all.',
      '## Keep the objective in view',
      'A finished board needs water at every drain, not necessarily through every pipe. When you are unsure whether a piece matters, ask whether it can connect the tap to a drain or complete a branch that does. If not, leave it alone while you secure the required network. This keeps the puzzle focused and protects time.',
      'Read the [Pipe Connect rules](/blogs/how-to-play-pipe-connect) for controls and the [timer strategy guide](/blogs/pipe-connect-tips-beat-the-timer) for a pre-move planning routine. Then practise the fixed-piece layout in [Pipe Connect](/play/pipe). If you prefer drawing routes directly, [Color Connect](/games/connect) uses paths without pipe rotations.',
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: 'Can Pipe Connect bolted pipes be rotated?', a: 'No. The dark bolted pieces are fixed and must be routed around.' },
      { q: 'Does every Pipe Connect piece need water?', a: 'No. Some pieces are decoys; you need to connect the tap to every drain.' },
      { q: 'How do I know which pipes connect?', a: 'Adjacent pieces connect only when both of their openings face one another.' },
      { q: 'Does undo restore time in Pipe Connect?', a: 'No. Undo reverses the last turn, but the elapsed time remains spent.' },
    ],
