      'When duplicates are allowed, a ring can refer to one copy while another copy of the same color is correctly placed. Think in terms of how many copies remain after the exact matches are accounted for. If the secret contains two copies and one position is fixed, only one copy needs to be found among the remaining slots.',
      '## Use the possible-code counter',
      'The possible-codes counter shows how many hidden rows still fit every clue you have seen. It is a useful check on your reasoning. If the count is small, compare the remaining candidate rows and choose a guess that tests a difference between them. A guess that repeats a color can be informative when the candidate set disagrees about whether that color appears twice.',
      'A guess already ruled out by a previous clue is marked red, so it cannot be the answer. Use a valid guess that gives information about the remaining possibilities. For the basic rules and other deduction habits, read the [Code Breaker rules guide](/blogs/how-to-play-mastermind); then practise duplicate handling in [Code Breaker](/play/code).',
      '## Keep a small record of each color',
      'A short table or note can prevent count errors. For each color, record whether it is absent, confirmed once, or may repeat. Then track any exact positions separately. This is especially helpful when the same color appears in multiple rows, because a ring on one guess may refer to a different copy than the filled dot in another.',
      'Update the count after every new clue rather than relying on memory. If one guess uses two copies and receives one match, the answer may have one copy, but another row might establish a second. Treat each clue as evidence that combines with earlier rows. The [possible-codes counter guide](/blogs/code-breaker-possible-codes-counter) shows how the remaining candidate set can verify your interpretation.',
      '## Avoid the most common duplicate-color mistake',
      'The usual error is to assume every copy of a color in your guess must be present in the code. Feedback only counts the copies the secret actually contains. A second mistake is to remove a color entirely after one guessed copy turns gray, even though a different copy may have earned a filled or ring clue. Count each color’s matches together with its position marks.',
      'Undo can refund an attempt for a guess that could not have been the secret, but its clue still counts. Use that feature when you spot an impossible row, not to remove a valid but inconvenient result. Hints reveal one position of the code, so save them for when several candidate patterns remain difficult to distinguish.',
      '## Make a controlled test, then update the board',
      'When a color count is uncertain, make one guess that tests it while preserving confirmed positions. Read the resulting feedback, update the count, then reconsider the candidate rows. With repeated colors, the exact number of copies is part of the puzzle, just like their locations. A clean record of both makes the later deductions much easier.',
      'Try a level in [Code Breaker](/play/code). Repeated colors arrive from level 10, so practise the distinct-color levels first and then track each color’s count alongside its position clues.',
