      'You do not need to calculate a perfect information score for every guess. A practical routine is enough: identify two or more candidates, find one difference, and make a valid guess that tests it. After feedback arrives, remove the rows that could not have produced that result. The counter should fall or at least confirm that your current possibilities remain broad.',
      '## Read the clue totals alongside the counter',
      'Filled dots mean correct color and position; rings mean correct color in another position. The clue does not identify which pegs produced the marks. Use the total with your candidate rows to infer what is fixed. If a row has one exact match and one misplaced match, candidates that create a different total are ruled out.',
      'For repeated colors, count copies as well as positions. A color used twice in a guess may occur only once in the secret, so one copy can be extra. The candidate counter is especially useful here because it applies the duplicate logic across all prior rows. Our [repeated-color Code Breaker guide](/blogs/code-breaker-repeated-colors) walks through that case in more detail.',
